Recursion means a function calls itself until a condition is met.

Simple definition:

"A function calling itself again and again until it reaches the base condition."

Every recursive function has 2 parts:

Base Case (Stopping condition)
Recursive Case (Function calls itself)

public class RecursionDemo {

    static void print(int n) {

        if (n == 0) {     // Base Case
            return;   // stop condition            }

        System.out.println(n);

        print(n - 1);     
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        print(5);

    }
}
Why do we use Recursion?

Some problems naturally break into smaller versions of themselves.

Instead of writing loops, recursion makes them easier to express.

Examples:

Factorial
Fibonacci
Binary Tree Traversal
Graph DFS
Merge Sort
Quick Sort

public class Factorial {

    static int fact(int n) {

        if (n == 1)
            return 1;

        return n * fact(n - 1);

    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        System.out.println(fact(5));

    }
}



